  "narrative": "**Narrative**\n\nTheRaMP enrichment analysis identified two highly significant pathways (FDR = 0.0) from the eight differentially-abundant metabolites. The dominant signal is **Eicosanoid Synthesis (WikiPathways WP167)** with a massive fold-enrichment of 1,251, driven by five matched compounds: arachidonic acid (C00219), leukotriene A4 (C00909), 5-HETE (C04805), 15-HETE (C04742), and 15-deoxy-Δ12,14-PGJ2 (C14717). The KEGG reaction graph confirms that arachidonic acid sits immediately upstream of both leukotriene A4 and 5-HETE (2-hop path), establishing a clear precursor–product cascade along the lipoxygenase and cycloxygenase branches of eicosanoid metabolism.\n\nThe second pathway, the **Selenium Micronutrient Network (WikiPathways WP15)**, connects six of the eight metabolites, notably pulling in L-cysteine (C00097) and L-cystathionine (C02291) alongside the eicosanoids. KEGG graph traversal confirms that L-cysteine is metabolically upstream of L-cystathionine (3-hop reversible path), linking sulfur amino acid catabolism to the broader network of selenium-dependent enzymes, particularly glutathione peroxidases that regulate oxidative tone during inflammatory bursts.\n\n**Biological significance.** Coordinated elevation of arachidonic acid, leukotriene A4, 5-HETE, 15-HETE, and 15-deoxy-PGJ2 indicates robust activation of both the 5-lipoxygenase and cyclooxygenase-2 pathways, consistent with an acute or chronic inflammatory stimulus. 15-Deoxy-PGJ2, a ligand for PPARγ, can drive anti-inflammatory transcriptional programs, while the HETEs serve as both effector lipids and substrates for further lipoxygenation. The simultaneous perturbation of the cysteine–cystathionine axis points to altered transsulfuration flux, likely redirecting cysteine toward glutathione synthesis to buffer the oxidative burden generated by eicosanoid overproduction. Mercaptopurine, though not enriched in these two pathways, is a purine antimetabolite whose differentiation signature warrants independent clinical consideration, particularly if the treatment condition involves immunosuppression or myelotoxicity.",
